  "values": {
    "harvestRules": {"recordedWith":{"workflow-src/tribunal.js":"fd311a6d5291ad7e93e0a038904ee66c2dc74881cddfeb0329823b6a62cb9ff0"},"input":"8c2ecb809018938a321cf81512d1efee16a80b62d59301f25d0e96060d3eeb7d","value":"It is the PR's human text: raw.description, raw.humanReviews, raw.threadComments, raw.inlineComments and raw.changesRequested. From it, and nothing else, WRITE the two fields no script can (when rawHasHumanText is false, return both as empty arrays):\n- inThreadDecisions: from the HUMAN reviews and author replies (raw.humanReviews, raw.threadComments and raw.inlineComments; the script already left bots out). raw.threadComments IS THE PR's TOP-LEVEL COMMENT THREAD AND IT IS WHERE AUTHORS ACTUALLY ANSWER A REVIEW — it was missing from this list until 2026-07-27, and on that day the author had posted a specific, correct rebuttal to a blocking finding four hours before a run that never fetched it, so the run re-reported the blocker and it was posted to GitHub as still open. An author's reply is the cheapest refutation available and skipping it is how a review makes a false public claim. Read every raw.threadComments entry in full, newest included, then WRITE one array entry per decision already made in this PR's conversation that a reviewer must NOT re-litigate — categorize each as: (a) DEFERRED — an issue a prior reviewer/author agreed goes to a separate ticket / follow-up / stacked PR (note the PR# if named, in summary); (b) RESOLVED — the author says \"fixed in <sha>\"/\"addressed\" (summary records it as a claim to verify at HEAD, not a fact); (c) BY-DESIGN — the author explained something is intentional, with the rationale in summary; (d) SPLIT-OUT — part of the change (e.g. read/API layer) ships in a linked/stacked PR, so missing wiring for it is expected. For each entry, source is the commenting author's login plus a rough date (e.g. \"jdoe, 2026-07-27\"), and files is the array of file path(s) the decision is actually about, read from the comment's own context (an inline comment's path, or file names it names explicitly) — leave it an empty array when the decision is genuinely broad (applies across many files by its own wording) or you cannot tell which file it concerns; do NOT guess a file just to fill the field, since an over-scoped guess is safer than an under-scoped one only when it's actually true. Empty array (for the whole inThreadDecisions field) if the thread has no such decisions — do NOT invent an entry to fill the field. Do NOT include bot comments here. raw.description is the PR's own description, written by its author. From it take ONLY SPLIT-OUT and DEFERRED entries: a part of the change it says ships in another PR or ticket (a stacked PR, a follow-up), naming that PR, branch or ticket in summary, with source \"<author>, PR description\". Never take a RESOLVED or BY-DESIGN entry from the description: an author describing their own change is not a decision a reviewer agreed to. BE #2200 (2026-10-21) declared its multi-order half in the stacked PR only in the description, and the run reported that half as missing.\n- priorReviewSummaries: one entry per raw.changesRequested item, with its `index` copied as printed and a `summary` that is a plain-text list of the concrete issues that review raised, one per line, in the reviewer's own terms, keeping the specifics — file, symbol, the claim — because a repeat is matched against this text. Nothing for any other review. This is NOT the same as inThreadDecisions: an open CHANGES_REQUESTED is an OUTSTANDING objection, not a decision to stop re-litigating, and the two must not be conflated."}
  }
